# Findings

## High — The attachment checks can start or replace the protected production daemon

Every `generation()` helper imports `createReconnectingMusicSessionClient`. That API is backed by the connect-or-start coordinator, so if PID `45621` disappears or the endpoint changes between a preceding check and the client call, the verifier can acquire the startup lease, clean a stale endpoint, and launch a new daemon. Phase 2 invokes it before any PID assertion and again after `bun run check`; Phase 3 has the same problem. This contradicts the run-wide requirement that a missing or changed baseline is a blocker and never authority to start, clean, or replace the unrelated production daemon.

Replace all live-evidence helpers with a strictly attachment-only operation, such as `createMusicSessionClient` against the already-validated socket (or a one-shot discovery API that is guaranteed never to start and whose cleanup capability is never invoked). Assert PID, command, socket identity/ownership, and expected generation around each observation so any disappearance or replacement fails closed. Apply this to the Phase 1 attachment checkpoint, Phase 2 pre/post gate, and all Phase 3 baseline/reload/exit comparisons.
